{
  "scenario": "Instruction: pick up the wooden cube and put it on the orange tray\nScene: A small wooden cube rests on the dark corrugated table surface toward the right-center of the workspace. To its left sits an orange rectangular tray. Open table space separates the cube from the tray. In the upper-left, a person is working on a laptop, and various cables and electronics occupy the lower-left corner of the table.",
  "experience": "Experience: Task: Pick up the wooden cube and place it on the orange tray. Scene: an isolated wooden cube on the right-center of a dark ribbed table, an empty orange tray to its left, with a person, laptop, camera, and cables in the upper/lower-left corners.\n\nSteps taken:\n1. Time_step 1 (PICK): Gripper was empty, so the robot chose to pick first. The cube was isolated with clear access, so no specific grasp section was required. Result: SUCCESS - the cube was grasped and lifted off the table; tray still empty; task not yet complete.\n2. Time_step 2 (PLACE): Gripper was holding the cube, so the robot chose to place. It placed the cube on the central, flat, stable section of the orange tray, deliberately avoiding the fragile laptop, camera, cables, and the person on the left. Result: SUCCESS - the cube rested on the tray center, nothing was knocked over or dropped; TASK COMPLETED.\n\nKey lessons learned:\n- Determine the next action from gripper state: if the gripper is EMPTY, PICK first; if it is HOLDING the target, PLACE next. Never attempt to place before a successful pick.\n- For an isolated object with clear access, no specific grasp section is needed; grasping a normal-sized cube directly works.\n- When placing, target a central, flat, stable section of the destination surface for stability.\n- Maintain awareness of fragile items (laptop, camera, cables) and any nearby person; route and place to avoid collisions or displacement.\n\nSimilar future scenarios and recommended sequencing: For any pick-and-place of a graspable object onto a tray/surface, (1) confirm gripper is empty and pick the isolated target, (2) verify the pick succeeded, (3) place at the destination's central stable region while avoiding fragile objects and people. Following this PICK-then-PLACE order with careful placement reliably completes the task without failure."
}

{
  "scenario": "Instruction: pick up the green cube and put it on the orange tray\nScene: A green cube sits on the dark ridged table surface in the right-center area. To its left, across an open stretch of table, lies a flat orange rectangular tray. Further left near the edge are a laptop, a camera, and cables, with a person seated behind them.",
  "experience": "Experience: Task: pick up the green cube and place it on the orange tray. The robot completed this in two steps. Step 1 (PICK): With an empty gripper, the robot reasoned that picking must come first. The green cube was isolated on the dark table with clear access, so it was grasped without needing a specific grasp section. Result: success, cube lifted off the table. Step 2 (PLACE): Now holding the cube, the robot reasoned that placing was the correct next action. It moved the cube left across the table to the orange tray and placed it at a central section for stability. Result: success, cube resting on tray, task completed with no objects knocked over or spilled and no harm to the nearby laptop, camera, cables, or seated person. Key lesson: For pick-and-place tasks, always sequence actions as PICK first (only when gripper is empty and target has clear access) then PLACE (only when an object is already grasped). Choose a central, flat, stable section of the target surface for placement. When transporting an object across the table past fragile or valuable items (laptops, cameras, cables) or a person, plan a path and placement that avoids collisions and spills. Similar scenarios: moving any small graspable object onto a tray, plate, or platform. Following the PICK-then-PLACE order and verifying the gripper state before each action prevents failures such as attempting to place without holding an object or attempting to pick when already holding one."
}

```

